{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/EF Core Transactions.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/EF Core Transactions.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ef core transactions ef core transactions","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/EF Core Transactions.md","fileName":"EF Core Transactions.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"EF Core Transactions","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-08 21:16\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Entity Framework Core Data Access]] [[ADO.NET Database Access]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Entity Framework Core Data Access","ADO.NET Database Access","hands-onobject-orientedprogrammingwithc.pdf","c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/EF Core Transactions.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/EF Core Transactions.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ef core transactions ef core transactions","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/EF Core Transactions.md","fileName":"EF Core Transactions.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"EF Core Transactions","heading":"EF Core Transactions","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A database transaction groups operations so they either succeed as a unit or are rolled back together. EF Core applies transactional behavior when saving a compatible group of changes and also permits explicit transaction control for broader workflows.\n\nTransactions protect consistency, but long-running transactions hold resources and can increase contention. A transaction boundary should therefore contain the smallest coherent business operation and define how failures are surfaced or retried.\n\nA database transaction is expected to be atomic, consistent, isolated, and durable: its grouped statements succeed as a unit, move data between valid states, avoid unsafe interference, and persist once committed. ADO.NET exposes explicit provider transactions for operations below an ORM boundary.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Entity Framework Core Data Access","ADO.NET Database Access","hands-onobject-orientedprogrammingwithc.pdf","c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/EF Core Transactions.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/EF Core Transactions.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"ef core transactions ef core transactions","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/EF Core Transactions.md","fileName":"EF Core Transactions.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"EF Core Transactions","heading":"References","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"[[hands-onobject-orientedprogrammingwithc.pdf]]\n\n[[c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Entity Framework Core Data Access","ADO.NET Database Access","hands-onobject-orientedprogrammingwithc.pdf","c80andnetcore30moderncross-platformdevelopment.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""}]}